1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:18,183
Most of us still use AI the old way. Something comes up, you kick off an agent, and then you babysit it. Every task starts with you. There's a new model: a harness that runs on its own, watches for what's being asked of you, and drafts the work before you ever ask.

2
00:00:19,183 --> 00:00:21,041
Muse, Grok Bot, and now dots.

3
00:00:22,041 --> 00:00:28,728
They all promise the same thing: an assistant that works while you're away, brings you finished work, and learns from your feedback.

4
00:00:29,728 --> 00:00:37,019
I've been running a small version of that on my laptop since before any of them launched. It's not a competitor. It's just a loop I find useful.

5
00:00:38,019 --> 00:00:39,180
I call it elves.

6
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:47,053
You know the story. The shoemaker goes to bed. In the morning, the shoes are finished. All he does is look them over.

7
00:00:48,053 --> 00:01:04,864
Imagine coming back from lunch, or a meeting, or a couple of hours of deep work, and every important ask that came in while you were away has already been drafted and is waiting for your review. No more triaging messages. No more kicking off agents and babysitting them.

8
00:01:05,864 --> 00:01:11,019
That's the whole idea. Every hour, it checks the inboxes and calendars I've told it about.

9
00:01:12,019 --> 00:01:16,106
Anything that needs real work gets drafted right away. Nothing is sent.

10
00:01:17,106 --> 00:01:24,165
After lunch, I open the review. Every item starts with what it did. I approve, edit, or toss it. That's my entire job.

11
00:01:25,165 --> 00:01:41,047
My reactions become rules. "Too long, Sam just wants the number" becomes a note on Sam's page, and the next draft is shorter. Over time, that adds up to a second brain: everything it learns about my work, written down in plain files I can read.

12
00:01:42,047 --> 00:01:52,078
What I like about it: it runs on my laptop, not in someone's cloud. It only reads what I've named. It never sends anything without me. And everything it learns is written down where I can read it.

13
00:01:53,078 --> 00:02:02,691
The whole product is one idea file. Hand it to whatever coding agent you already have, even the one you're allowed to use at work. It builds it, and you can customize it however you like.

14
00:02:03,691 --> 00:02:05,456
It's on GitHub. Link in the post.
